    <item>
      <title>MeshXFS: AI Adapts XFS Into A Clustered File-System</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/MeshXFS-XFS-Clustered</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/MeshXFS-XFS-Clustered</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 17 Oct 2026 17:02:40 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX STORAGE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxstorage.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; MeshXFS is a clustered open-source file-system in which "no human wrote any of its own code". Depending upon your views of AI, not necessarily something you want to hear for entrusting your data in an AI-created solution. Instead, Claude Opus was tasked with adapting the XFS file-system driver into a clustered file-system. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Steve Shoecraft who started the MeshXFS project and wrote into Phoronix to highlight the initiative. He remarked: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"MeshXFS began as an experiment to answer one question: can AI write a kernel filesystem driver, and not a simple one, but a clustered filesystem with distributed locking, concurrent access from many nodes, fencing and crash recovery? It is now a working, released, open-source (GPL-2.0) POSIX clustered filesystem, and the answer so far is yes."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;XFS was adapted into a POSIX clustered file-system complete with distributed locking, many node concurrent access capabilities, fencing and crash recovery, and other features. Steve further explained of this MeshXFS creation: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"It starts from the upstream XFS code, written by the XFS developers. Everything that turns XFS into a filesystem many machines can mount read-write at once was written by AI: &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- two distributed lock managers: one over the network, and one that keeps lock state on the shared disk itself using SCSI COMPARE AND WRITE, with no lock network at all &lt;br /&gt;- cache coherency across nodes, fencing with SCSI persistent reservations, and survivor-led journal recovery &lt;br /&gt;- an on-disk envelope around XFS, plus the userspace tools (mkfs, fsck, resize) &lt;br /&gt;- every change to the forked XFS code since the fork (the xfs/ tree has grown from about 226k to about 314k lines) &lt;br /&gt;- the test harnesses, the test rig automation, and the release validation &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Claude Opus, running as an autonomous coding agent in Claude Code, did a large share of the work, consulting Claude Fable, GPT, Gemini and Astra on the hardest problems: lock protocol design, recovery races, fencing correctness. The AI also wrote and ran the tests, diagnosed the failures and fixed them. My part was setting the goal and the bar: data integrity first, never crash the host, and stay within twice the time native XFS takes on the same storage. I did not write, edit or review the code."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For those concerned about entrusting their data into an AI-modified file-system, each MeshXFS release is said to undergo a ~30 row suite on real multi-node clusters through a variety of different real-world scenarios. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Those curious about MeshXFS and wanting to learn more can do so via the project's &lt;a href="https://github.com/sshoecraft/meshxfs"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Linux Lands Fix for Rare, Varying SMP Boot Hangs Affecting Kernels Since 2024</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-KThreads-NBCON</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-KThreads-NBCON</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:54:33 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX KERNEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxkernel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Among the flurry of last minute patch activity ahead of tomorrow's presumed &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Linux+7.3"&gt;Linux 7.3&lt;/a&gt; stable kernel release is a fix for addressing some rare hangs at boot time. The reports varied but ultimately tracked down to a kernel change made two years ago. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Various bug reports have come about for common place SMP systems hanging on boot and was ultimately narrowed down to the non-blocking console "NBCON" code. To avoid these boot hangs, moving the start of the NBCON console printing KThreads to later in the boot process avoids these possible system hangs. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Linutronix engineer John Ogness explained in the now-merged &lt;a href="https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/commit/?id=5f339c46f153184570f16c8e789c9709b14cbe8b"&gt;fix&lt;/a&gt; of postponing the KThread start-up: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"There have been various bug reports related to SMP systems hanging on boot. The reports vary but seem to have some overlap with regard to missed IPI's during SMP setup. The reports also show that reverting NBCON console usage allows the bugs to disappear. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The NBCON kthreads for console printing are started very early during boot using early_initcall(). Since these kthreads are asynchronously enabling/disabling interrupts during early boot, they may be interfering with early SMP setup on some hardware platforms. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Starting the console printing kthreads later has shown to avoid the hanging on systems that could previously reproduce the issue. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Postpone kthread starting until after all arch initcalls have completed."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;This is treated as a fix for Linux kernel code present since September 2024 when printer KThreads were first introduced in the printk() code. Thus will presumably be back-ported in the days ahead from Linux 7.3 to other currently supported kernel branches.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>GNOME's New Local/Cloud AI Chat App Gains More Features</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-Moose-0.6-AI</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-Moose-0.6-AI</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:39:25 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="GNOME" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/gnome.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Coming about the past few months has been &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-Moose-Local-AI"&gt;Moose as a GNOME-aligned AI chat application&lt;/a&gt; built around GTK4 and libadwaita. It began as a local AI chat app for the GNOME desktop and grew to support cloud AI services as well. This past week marked the latest feature release for Moose. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Moose 0.6 adds support for secure cloud connections with protected credential storage for those wanting to make use of cloud AI platforms. There is also now a Local-Only mode for using Ollama managed by Moose on your own device. Permission handling can also now be set per-chat or remembers across chats of the same provider. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=moose_06_1" alt="Moose 0.6" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The updated GNOME AI chat app also now has a GNOME Files browser for interacting with files on your system and attaching them to an AI chat conversation. There is also an expanded local model catalog for chat / vision / reasoning / coding purposes.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=moose_06_2" alt="Moose 0.6 settings" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Moose 0.6.1 was also released with some follow-on changes, including support for connecting to Mistral AI and DeepSeek from Moose. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Moose can be downloaded from &lt;a href="https://github.com/moooossee/moose/releases#release-v0.6.0"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt; and also available in Flatpak form on Flathub. More details on the new Moose release and other GNOME app updates for the past week can be found via &lt;a href="https://thisweek.gnome.org/posts/2026/10/twig-270/"&gt;This Week in GNOME&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Linux 7.3 Lands Last-Minute Fixes For Samsung 990 PRO &amp; 990 EVO Plus SSDs</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-Samsung-990-Immediate</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-Samsung-990-Immediate</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:15:12 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX STORAGE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxstorage.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Ahead of the Linux 7.3 stable kernel expected to be released tomorrow, last minute workarounds were merged for the Samsung 990 PRO and Samsung 990 EVO Plus NVMe solid state drives. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Coming by way of the Linux 7.3 PCI fixes pull,  the Linux kernel will now ignore the Samsung 990 PRO and 990 EVO Plus NVMe SSDs from advertising their support for Immediate Readiness. PCIe 4.0 introduced the Immediate Readiness bit for indicating the drive can be instantly ready for handling configuration requests following a reset. This improves how quickly the OS can interact with an SSD following a reset. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;While Immediate Readiness is a nice feature, it turns out at least some firmware versions for the Samsung 990 PRO and 990 EVO Plus are advertising the feature without actually supporting it. That in turn is causing problems for Linux and also breaking VFIO pass-through for these drives. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=samsung_990_pro_med" alt="Samsung 990 PRO" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/commit/?id=0f8b5d90d179b9639be1dbeff59d6d971e6b0527"&gt;The fix&lt;/a&gt; now merged to Linux 7.3 Git explains: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"The Samsung 990 PRO [144d:a80c] advertises Immediate Readiness, so pcie_flr() doesn't wait after FLR, but the drive returns ~0 for up to 7 ms. Since commit 10baa9b4df40 ("PCI: Drop unnecessary retries when restoring BARs"), the config restore after FLR is lost, which breaks VFIO passthrough of the drive.  Firmware 8B2QJXD7 responds right after FLR, but older firmware such as 1B2QJXD7 doesn't, and the firmware version isn't visible here. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;[bhelgaas: The Samsung 990 EVO Plus [144d:a80d] with 2B2QKXG7 firmware seems to have the same problem.]"&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;So by way of the &lt;a href="https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/commit/?id=4766082b791b515953d87f7a24c2ea857e6c10b0"&gt;PCI merge&lt;/a&gt; overnight, the issue is fixed in time for Linux 7.3 stable.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>KDE Plasma 6.9 To Debut New Weather Widget &amp; On-Screen Keyboard</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.9-Weather-Keyboard</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.9-Weather-Keyboard</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:01:15 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="KDE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/kde.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; With &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.8-Released"&gt;KDE Plasma 6.8 released&lt;/a&gt; earlier this week, KDE developers have begun working on more feature activity toward Plasma 6.9.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;New this week is a overhauled weather widget. Plasma's weather widget is nicely redone with an improved layout and more information on the weather forecast obtained via Open-Meteo. Overall looks to be a solid design improvement over its older weather widget:&lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=plasma_69_weather" alt="Plasma 6.9's new weather widget" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Another big ticket item for Plasma 6.9 is landing a re-engineered on-screen keyboard. The new Plasma on-screen keyboard includes the option of having a full-size keyboard, a Japanese flick keyboard, and distinct layouts not tied to system locales:  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=plasma_69_keyboard" alt="Plasma 6.9's new on-screen keyboard" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Plus there have been a number of bug fixes and other improvements for Plasma 6.9 as well as preparing for the Plasma 6.8.1 bug-fix point release. All the details on the exciting Plasma developments this week via &lt;a href="https://blogs.kde.org/2026/10/17/this-week-in-plasma-new-weather-widget-and-on-screen-keyboard/"&gt;This Week in Plasma&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Ubuntu Touch 24.04-2.1 Brings New Hardware Support &amp; Important Security Fix</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Ubuntu-Touch-24.04-2.1</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Ubuntu-Touch-24.04-2.1</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 17 Oct 2026 09:49:26 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="UBUNTU" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/ubuntu.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Ubuntu Touch 24.04-2.1 was released on Friday as the latest update to this community-maintained tablet/smartphone Ubuntu Linux platform. Ubuntu Touch 24.04-1.5 was also released as a maintenance update for the prior series. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Both of these Ubuntu Touch releases shipped with a security fix for vulnerabilities that allowed unconfined applications to gain administrator privileges without passwords. The issue arose as any unconfined application can communicate with system-level lomiri-download-manager and lomiri-upload-manager via D-Bus to execute arbitrary commands or read any file on the device as root. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2024&amp;image=ubuntu_touch_4" alt="Ubuntu Touch demo" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Ubuntu Touch 24.04-2.1 is exciting for expanding the supported device list. Newly supported hardware by Ubuntu Touch includes the Nothing Phone (1), OnePlus Nord N10, OnePlus Nord N100, Volla Tablet, Xiaomi Redmi Note 9 Pro (Global), Xiaomi Redmi Note 9S/9 Pro (India), Xiaomi Redmi Note 9 Pro Max, and Xiaomi Poco M2 Pro. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Ubuntu Touch also updated its Morph Browser engine against a newer QtWebEngine build, and brought various other fixes and minor improvements. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on the updated Ubuntu Touch 24.04 base via &lt;a href="https://ubports.com/blog/ubports-news-1/ubuntu-touch-24-04-2-1-and-24-04-1-5-release-4014"&gt;UBports.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Wine 11.20 Released With CD-ROM Support Change</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-11.20-Released</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-11.20-Released</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:44:06 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="WINE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/wine.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Wine 11.20 is out today as the newest bi-weekly development release of this software for enjoying Windows games and apps on Linux. We are quickly approaching the point where this development work will culminate into the Wine 12.0 stable release in early 2027. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Wine 11.20 provides the latest batch of feature work and fixes toward Wine 12. Wine 11.20 continues recent work around ARM64EC support to now include replay debugging capabilities. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Today's release also brings improvements to XML validation. Another notable change is Wine moving its CD-ROM support code over to its Mount Manager. The CD-ROM change was made as part of an NTDLL code rework for no longer opening Unix devices directly and instead relying on the Mount Manager "mountmgr" code. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Wine 11.20 also provides 24 bug fixes affecting software from QuickBooks to games like Battlefield 1942 and Pure Pinball. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Wine 11.20 downloads and more details via &lt;a href="https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/releases/wine-11.20"&gt;WineHQ.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>17 More Security Vulnerabilities Made Public For X.Org Libraries</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/17-Vulnerabilities-Xorg-Libs</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/17-Vulnerabilities-Xorg-Libs</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:20:39 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="X.ORG" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/xorg.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Just shy of two weeks since &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/X.Org-12-New-Issues-2026"&gt;a dozen vulnerabilities were published for the X.Org Server and XWayland&lt;/a&gt;, more than one dozen vulnerabilities were made public today affecting multiple X.Org client libraries. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Seventeen vulnerabilities were made public today affecting the libX11 X11 library, libXi for X Input, libXpm for X Pixmap format support, libXrenderer for X RENDER, and libXtst for the X TEST extension. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The vulnerabilities found by different researchers are predominantly out-of-bounds reads and writes as well as some heap buffer overflows and possible denial of service. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Updated versions include libX11 1.8.14, libXi 1.8.4, libXpm 3.5.20, libXrender 0.9.13, and libXtst 1.2.6. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on today's disclosures via the &lt;a href="https://lists.x.org/archives/xorg/2026-October/062301.html"&gt;X.Org mailing list&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>GNOME OS Preview Announced In Road Toward A Stable Release</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-OS-Preview</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-OS-Preview</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:37:11 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="GNOME" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/gnome.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; The &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/GNOME+OS"&gt;GNOME OS&lt;/a&gt; Linux distribution to date has just been in GNOME OS Nightly form for tracking the latest nightly packages of GNOME in their current development form. GNOME OS Preview was announced today as their trek toward a stable GNOME OS in using the latest GNOME stable releases rather than nightly form. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;GNOME developers are working toward making GNOME OS a end-user general purpose OS rather than just a nightly testing artifact. Particularly with enhancements GNOME OS has been seeing around systemd-homed and systemd-sysupdate and other modern tech features, there is increased appeal in making it available to a wider audience. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2026&amp;image=gnome_os_preview_1_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=gnome_os_preview_1_med" alt="GNOME OS Preview" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The initial GNOME OS Preview is tracking the latest GNOME 51.1 components. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2026&amp;image=gnome_os_preview_2_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=gnome_os_preview_2_med" alt="GNOME OS Preview desktop" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;In my initial brief testing of GNOME OS Preview it has been working out well. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2026&amp;image=gnome_os_preview_3_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=gnome_os_preview_3_med" alt="GNOME OS Preview apps" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Those wanting to learn more about the GNOME OS Preview can do so via &lt;a href="https://blogs.gnome.org/adrianvovk/2026/10/16/gnome-os-preview/"&gt;this blog post&lt;/a&gt;. GNOME OS Preview and Nightly ISOs can be downloaded from &lt;a href="https://os.gnome.org/"&gt;os.gnome.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>The Best Features Of Linux 7.3: Performance Improvements, Lots From Valve</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-Best-Features</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-Best-Features</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:17:47 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX KERNEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxkernel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; The &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Linux+7.3"&gt;Linux 7.3&lt;/a&gt; kernel is slated to be officially released on Sunday, 18 October, barring any last minute issues from coming about. With that said, here's a reminder about some of the most exciting features to look forward to in this next kernel version. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Exciting me the most about Linux 7.3 have been &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/linux-73-amd-epyc"&gt;some nice I/O performance improvements&lt;/a&gt; that deliver some &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/linux-73-panther-lake"&gt;solid gains&lt;/a&gt; both for synthetic I/O benchmarks and carrying over to databases and other heavy I/O work. These I/O wins with Linux 7.3 have carried across &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-Threadripper-9995WX"&gt;various systems&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Separately and also on the performance side are some &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/linux-73-btrfs"&gt;nice Btrfs performance improvements in Linux 7.3&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Linux 7.3 also continues preparing for upcoming AMD Zen 6 platforms with now identifying the new low-power CPU core type complementing the typical performance and dense cores. The AMD HSMP driver and other platform drivers have also added support for upcoming Zen 6 client processors. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Another exciting hardware addition is &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/M3-Pro-Max-Ultra-Linux-7.3"&gt;initial Apple M3 Pro / Max / Ultra support&lt;/a&gt; to complement the base M3 model added in Linux 7.2. But this code isn't yet ready for end-users and is just early bring-up. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For  those still running early generations of AMD GCN GPUs, Timur Kristóf of Valve's Linux graphics team has landed &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-AMDGPU-GFX6-Modifiers"&gt;DRM format modifier support for old AMD GPUs&lt;/a&gt; as well as &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-GFX7-Soft-Reset-Linux-7.3"&gt;improving GPU reset recovery support&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Also on the topic of old/low-end graphics hardware, Linux 7.3 brings the change for &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-Improving-vRAM-Mgmt"&gt;improving vRAM management on systems with limited amounts of video memory&lt;/a&gt;. Another Linux win thanks to Valve. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=new_steam_controller_med" alt="Steam Controller" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For Linux gamers, further excitement with Linux 7.3 is &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-HID"&gt;initial kernel driver support for the 2026 Steam Controller&lt;/a&gt;. There is also a &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/MSI-Claw-Gamepad-Linux-7.3"&gt;MSI Claw gamepad driver&lt;/a&gt; that was worked on by Valve. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on the many features of this next kernel version can be found via our &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/linux-73-features"&gt;Linux 7.3 feature overview&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
